                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1a2b9233-78a0-3b6d-91ef-4848f37f33f9",
                                        "entry_id": "50d74033-5a17-3bcd-9663-c5cb7f51133a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To form a cope or arch; to arch or bend; to bow."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "62847a5c-8663-4dc8-b83d-b0380cc3a7e3",
                                                "sense_id": "1a2b9233-78a0-3b6d-91ef-4848f37f33f9",
                                                "text": "[W]e ſee that wreſtlers onely doe claſpe about, and imbrace one another with their armes; and the moſt part of their ſtriving one againſt another, whether it be performed by taking hold either directly or indirectly, by tripping, by coping and tugging, doe all bring them together, and enterlace them; […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "cope",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2ce1b8e7-ca46-31e2-9a19-d3498886e3e1",
                                        "entry_id": "c0f6dac7-604e-3824-a7fe-dec79f931de3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To bargain for; to buy."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0adb52c1-c095-396d-a8f0-07a184e80feb",
                                        "entry_id": "c0f6dac7-604e-3824-a7fe-dec79f931de3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To exchange or barter."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "48f24125-12da-4f76-bf66-9da5a7e88661",
                                                "sense_id": "0adb52c1-c095-396d-a8f0-07a184e80feb",
                                                "text": "[The Patron] Will cope with thee in reasonable wise;\nThat if the living yerely doo arise\nTo fortie pound, that then his yongest sonne\nShall twentie have, and twentie thou hast wonne."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5542130e-4fbb-3fd2-8816-fa7671f56127",
                                        "entry_id": "c0f6dac7-604e-3824-a7fe-dec79f931de3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To make return for; to requite; to repay."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "eb8a46b5-e160-4a22-abbf-529403c14dc8",
                                                "sense_id": "5542130e-4fbb-3fd2-8816-fa7671f56127",
                                                "text": "Three thousand ducats due unto the Jew, / We freely cope your courteous pains withal."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4a4d903f-88f5-378e-a03a-55bc24e15f1b",
                                        "entry_id": "c0f6dac7-604e-3824-a7fe-dec79f931de3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To match oneself against; to meet; to encounter."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5ad1c498-b920-4077-a994-cd0876c02514",
                                                "sense_id": "4a4d903f-88f5-378e-a03a-55bc24e15f1b",
                                                "text": "I love to cope him in these sullen fits."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1baa9f1f-8fa9-427c-95ac-65665807149b",
                                                "sense_id": "4a4d903f-88f5-378e-a03a-55bc24e15f1b",
                                                "text": "They say he yesterday coped Hector in the battle, and struck him down."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b7a181b7-c26e-4b75-bcb2-08c2ff6f6b52",
                                                "sense_id": "4a4d903f-88f5-378e-a03a-55bc24e15f1b",
                                                "text": "Says, ‘Mistress, do you travel to be coped? / Give me my fee: for sure, a plump-cheeked lass / Shall not the porter's lodge unkissèd pass.’"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8aa80267-d394-42ae-950f-301c82a49007",
                                                "sense_id": "4a4d903f-88f5-378e-a03a-55bc24e15f1b",
                                                "text": "Host coped with host, dire was the din of war."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "20ce54a5-ef5e-3f5c-885a-c2741eccf767",
                                        "entry_id": "c0f6dac7-604e-3824-a7fe-dec79f931de3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To encounter; to meet; to have to do with."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d436da52-d557-4ee4-b8e9-8f8c98a57e79",
                                                "sense_id": "20ce54a5-ef5e-3f5c-885a-c2741eccf767",
                                                "text": "Horatio, thou art e'en as just a man / As e'er my conversation coped withal."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "cope",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8b27c28d-c7e1-3a61-8b6b-bb745713920d",
                                        "entry_id": "62396e1c-adb2-3df6-a867-ba8b3a349529",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To tie or sew up the mouth of a ferret used for hunting rabbits."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "dialectal",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ba86f3fc-ab53-4e86-a684-0824b9262c23",
                                                "sense_id": "8b27c28d-c7e1-3a61-8b6b-bb745713920d",
                                                "text": "His nimble ferrets must now become pioners for their master who coupes them, lest they should grow too fat to endure labour."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4aed01f4-517f-433a-b3e8-e2e9c07f2b04",
                                                "sense_id": "8b27c28d-c7e1-3a61-8b6b-bb745713920d",
                                                "text": "The use of this word is confined to warreners, who are said to 'cope' their ferrets, when they sew or tie up their mouths, to prevent them from biting rabbits, when they are used to drive them from their holes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a27d10bb-3d31-33ba-8280-da51c75872a4",
                                        "entry_id": "62396e1c-adb2-3df6-a867-ba8b3a349529",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To silence or prevent from speaking."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "figuratively",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9e596254-3058-443f-be2b-ec23ab4501bd",
                                                "sense_id": "a27d10bb-3d31-33ba-8280-da51c75872a4",
                                                "text": "Well sir? how triflingly soeuer you trauers the matter, these my Philosophicall proceedings (for any thing hitherto heard) might fullie suffice to put your fantasticall fooleries to a perpetuall non-sute: were you not like to the rauenous Ferret, which rendeth in peeces whatsoeuer poore Rabbet doth come in her reach. And therefore it shall not be amisse to cope vp your lips a little, by taking foorthwith so strict a course as you shall neuer be able to contradict with all your skill: which may in this sort be verie fitly effected."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8bd4e2a8-acb2-4f79-95d6-a5f0034bc299",
                                                "sense_id": "a27d10bb-3d31-33ba-8280-da51c75872a4",
                                                "text": "And tell me Signior, why when you eate our good cheare i'th City, haue you handſome wide chops, but meeting vs at Court, none; your gumme's glew'd vp, your lips coap'd like a Ferret, not ſo much as the corner of a Cuſtard; if a cold cup, and a dry cheate loaf 'tis well."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "37b102e0-f958-440f-9778-e828305cc256",
                                                "sense_id": "a27d10bb-3d31-33ba-8280-da51c75872a4",
                                                "text": "That is; because Roger has a vocal instrument between his chin and his nose, called a mouth, and being not muzled, gagged or cop'd; but having a free power, faculty or Page 127 May to open it, and order it as he think fit; therefore he May stretch it out as wide as he please, and swear quite cross the Island, that he'l have the whole, or at least half:"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
